1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,280
Ines is a sculptor, and tonight she's sketching

2
00:00:03,360 --> 00:00:06,715
a heron in pencil, so the tip leaves a soft

3
00:00:06,795 --> 00:00:08,355
gray line on the paper.

4
00:00:09,096 --> 00:00:11,736
Then she picks up a scribe with a tiny diamond

5
00:00:11,776 --> 00:00:14,331
at its tip, and she scratches that same

6
00:00:14,411 --> 00:00:16,971
outline into a plate of hard steel.

7
00:00:17,771 --> 00:00:19,878
She has one night to mark this heron and

8
00:00:19,958 --> 00:00:22,494
cut it out, because the town unveils it

9
00:00:22,534 --> 00:00:24,364
in the morning.

10
00:00:24,444 --> 00:00:27,434
The gray in her pencil is mostly graphite,

11
00:00:27,514 --> 00:00:30,504
and graphite is pure carbon.

12
00:00:30,624 --> 00:00:33,605
Her diamond is pure carbon too.

13
00:00:33,685 --> 00:00:36,085
So how can the same atom be soft enough

14
00:00:36,165 --> 00:00:37,845
to rub off on paper...

15
00:00:37,925 --> 00:00:40,165
and hard enough to scratch steel?

16
00:00:41,580 --> 00:00:43,140
By the end of this video,

17
00:00:43,220 --> 00:00:45,620
you'll look at almost any atom in a molecule

18
00:00:45,700 --> 00:00:49,260
and name its hybridization in a few seconds.

19
00:00:49,380 --> 00:00:51,500
Along the way, you'll see why carbon makes

20
00:00:51,580 --> 00:00:54,360
four bonds, where double and triple bonds

21
00:00:54,440 --> 00:00:57,161
come from, and what makes Ines's pencil

22
00:00:57,241 --> 00:01:00,501
and her diamond so different.

23
00:01:00,581 --> 00:01:02,981
So why does one little label deserve this

24
00:01:03,061 --> 00:01:05,061
much attention?

25
00:01:05,141 --> 00:01:07,201
Tell me an atom's hybridization,

26
00:01:07,241 --> 00:01:09,361
and I can tell you which way its bonds point

27
00:01:09,441 --> 00:01:11,870
and the angles between them.

28
00:01:11,924 --> 00:01:13,737
I can also tell you whether that atom can

29
00:01:13,817 --> 00:01:16,917
make a double bond or a triple bond.

30
00:01:16,997 --> 00:01:19,477
You'll lean on hybridization in almost every

31
00:01:19,677 --> 00:01:22,037
chapter of organic chemistry from here on.

32
00:01:25,837 --> 00:01:28,157
We ended the last video with a problem.

33
00:01:28,872 --> 00:01:31,632
Carbon's outer shell holds four electrons.

34
00:01:32,333 --> 00:01:34,909
One pair fills the two s orbital,

35
00:01:34,989 --> 00:01:37,069
and the other two electrons sit unpaired

36
00:01:37,149 --> 00:01:38,429
in p orbitals.

37
00:01:39,109 --> 00:01:41,509
An unpaired electron is what an atom uses

38
00:01:41,549 --> 00:01:44,625
to make a bond, so carbon should make just

39
00:01:44,705 --> 00:01:47,005
two bonds.

40
00:01:47,045 --> 00:01:48,890
But then you look at methane,

41
00:01:48,970 --> 00:01:52,420
which is one carbon holding four hydrogens.

42
00:01:52,500 --> 00:01:56,901
It has four bonds, and all four are identical.

43
00:01:56,981 --> 00:01:59,301
In this drawing, the solid wedge is a bond

44
00:01:59,381 --> 00:02:01,461
coming toward you, and the striped one is

45
00:02:01,621 --> 00:02:03,791
a bond going away.

46
00:02:03,831 --> 00:02:06,311
Every pair of those bonds meets at the same

47
00:02:06,391 --> 00:02:08,551
angle, which is one hundred nine point five

48
00:02:08,711 --> 00:02:10,551
degrees.

49
00:02:10,631 --> 00:02:13,621
So where did the two extra bonds come from,

50
00:02:13,701 --> 00:02:17,047
and why do all four match?

51
00:02:17,087 --> 00:02:19,629
The answer comes in two steps,

52
00:02:19,655 --> 00:02:22,984
and the first step is called promotion.

53
00:02:23,010 --> 00:02:25,677
An electron leaves the full two s orbital

54
00:02:25,757 --> 00:02:29,247
and moves up into the empty p orbital.

55
00:02:29,287 --> 00:02:31,887
Now carbon has four unpaired electrons,

56
00:02:31,927 --> 00:02:35,617
with one in each of its four outer orbitals.

57
00:02:35,657 --> 00:02:38,753
That move costs energy, but the two extra

58
00:02:38,793 --> 00:02:40,873
bonds give back far more energy than the

59
00:02:40,913 --> 00:02:44,033
move cost, so it's worth it every time carbon

60
00:02:44,073 --> 00:02:46,220
bonds.

61
00:02:46,246 --> 00:02:47,873
There's still a problem, though,

62
00:02:47,953 --> 00:02:51,153
because those four orbitals aren't alike.

63
00:02:51,233 --> 00:02:53,233
One is a round s orbital,

64
00:02:53,313 --> 00:02:55,993
and three are p orbitals shaped like dumbbells,

65
00:02:56,033 --> 00:02:58,698
set at right angles to each other.

66
00:02:58,738 --> 00:03:00,578
If carbon bonded with them just as they

67
00:03:00,658 --> 00:03:03,058
are, three bonds would sit ninety degrees

68
00:03:03,178 --> 00:03:05,138
apart, and the fourth would be different

69
00:03:05,218 --> 00:03:07,013
from the rest.

70
00:03:07,053 --> 00:03:09,480
Methane doesn't look like that at all.

71
00:03:09,586 --> 00:03:11,724
So this is the second step,

72
00:03:11,764 --> 00:03:14,685
and you can think of it like mixing paint.

73
00:03:14,754 --> 00:03:17,234
If you pour one can of red and three cans

74
00:03:17,314 --> 00:03:19,421
of white into a tub and stir,

75
00:03:19,447 --> 00:03:21,287
you can fill four cans again,

76
00:03:21,341 --> 00:03:24,939
and now every can holds the same pink.

77
00:03:24,979 --> 00:03:27,112
Carbon does that with its orbitals.

78
00:03:27,166 --> 00:03:29,539
It mixes the one s orbital and the three

79
00:03:29,619 --> 00:03:32,339
p orbitals into four new orbitals that are

80
00:03:32,419 --> 00:03:34,499
all identical.

81
00:03:34,539 --> 00:03:36,592
We call them hybrid orbitals,

82
00:03:36,646 --> 00:03:39,179
and this kind is named sp³,

83
00:03:39,259 --> 00:03:42,219
because it's made from one s and three p.

84
00:03:42,299 --> 00:03:45,955
Four orbitals went in, and four came out.

85
00:03:45,995 --> 00:03:48,185
Mixing changes their shape,

86
00:03:48,225 --> 00:03:51,650
but it never changes how many there are.

87
00:03:51,690 --> 00:03:53,970
Each hybrid has one big lobe,

88
00:03:54,010 --> 00:03:56,250
and since every lobe holds electrons and

89
00:03:56,330 --> 00:03:57,983
electrons repel each other,

90
00:03:58,037 --> 00:04:00,250
the four lobes push as far apart as they

91
00:04:00,330 --> 00:04:01,850
can.

92
00:04:01,930 --> 00:04:03,770
That points them at the four corners of

93
00:04:03,930 --> 00:04:06,490
a tetrahedron, a shape with four corners

94
00:04:06,610 --> 00:04:09,157
and four triangle faces.

95
00:04:09,183 --> 00:04:11,970
And the angle between any two of them is...

96
00:04:12,010 --> 00:04:14,450
one hundred nine point five degrees,

97
00:04:14,490 --> 00:04:17,170
which is exactly what methane shows.

98
00:04:17,210 --> 00:04:19,770
Now we can look inside the diamond on Ines's

99
00:04:19,850 --> 00:04:21,450
scribe.

100
00:04:21,570 --> 00:04:25,030
Every carbon atom in it is sp³.

101
00:04:25,110 --> 00:04:27,750
Each carbon uses its four hybrids to bond

102
00:04:27,870 --> 00:04:30,070
to four more carbons, and each of those

103
00:04:30,150 --> 00:04:32,150
carbons bonds to four more.

104
00:04:32,946 --> 00:04:35,586
The pattern runs on in every direction with

105
00:04:35,666 --> 00:04:37,826
strong bonds all the way through,

106
00:04:37,906 --> 00:04:39,986
so there's no weak layer anywhere that could

107
00:04:40,066 --> 00:04:40,866
slip.

108
00:04:41,506 --> 00:04:43,378
That's why the diamond tip bites into the

109
00:04:43,458 --> 00:04:46,458
steel, and the steel can't scratch it back.

110
00:04:47,958 --> 00:04:48,998
Now it's your turn.

111
00:04:49,798 --> 00:04:52,278
Suppose an atom mixes its s orbital with

112
00:04:52,358 --> 00:04:55,078
only two of its three p orbitals.

113
00:04:55,158 --> 00:04:57,918
How many hybrid orbitals does it get?

114
00:04:58,038 --> 00:05:00,318
And how many p orbitals are left over,

115
00:05:00,398 --> 00:05:01,198
unmixed?

116
00:05:08,441 --> 00:05:11,529
One s plus two p gives three orbitals going

117
00:05:11,561 --> 00:05:14,161
in, so three hybrids come out.

118
00:05:14,921 --> 00:05:17,401
The atom started with three p orbitals and

119
00:05:17,481 --> 00:05:20,681
it used two, so one p orbital is left over.

120
00:05:21,481 --> 00:05:24,228
The trap is thinking that mixing uses orbitals

121
00:05:24,308 --> 00:05:27,188
up, when the number going in always equals

122
00:05:27,228 --> 00:05:29,414
the number coming out.

123
00:05:29,494 --> 00:05:31,894
And that leftover p orbital is about to

124
00:05:32,054 --> 00:05:33,254
matter a lot.

125
00:05:37,054 --> 00:05:39,294
An atom doesn't have to mix all three of

126
00:05:39,374 --> 00:05:42,069
its p orbitals, and you'll see in a minute

127
00:05:42,149 --> 00:05:44,544
why it would hold one back.

128
00:05:44,624 --> 00:05:47,160
When it mixes the s with two of them,

129
00:05:47,200 --> 00:05:49,975
it gets three hybrids, and they're called

130
00:05:50,055 --> 00:05:52,055
sp².

131
00:05:52,215 --> 00:05:54,680
Those three lobes spread out flat,

132
00:05:54,760 --> 00:05:56,901
one hundred twenty degrees apart,

133
00:05:56,981 --> 00:05:58,821
like the blades of a ceiling fan.

134
00:05:59,536 --> 00:06:02,016
The one leftover p orbital stands straight

135
00:06:02,096 --> 00:06:04,071
up through that flat plane,

136
00:06:04,151 --> 00:06:06,711
with one lobe above it and one below.

137
00:06:07,822 --> 00:06:10,382
An atom can also mix the s with just one

138
00:06:10,462 --> 00:06:12,167
p orbital.

139
00:06:12,247 --> 00:06:14,947
Then it gets only a pair of hybrids,

140
00:06:14,987 --> 00:06:17,322
and they're called plain sp,

141
00:06:17,402 --> 00:06:19,202
with no number.

142
00:06:19,322 --> 00:06:21,442
Those lobes get as far apart as they can

143
00:06:21,562 --> 00:06:23,993
by pointing in opposite directions,

144
00:06:24,073 --> 00:06:27,013
so the angle is one hundred eighty degrees,

145
00:06:27,053 --> 00:06:28,873
a straight line.

146
00:06:28,953 --> 00:06:32,339
This time two p orbitals are left over.

147
00:06:32,419 --> 00:06:34,699
They stand at right angles to that line,

148
00:06:34,739 --> 00:06:36,899
and at right angles to each other.

149
00:06:37,099 --> 00:06:39,779
So what are the leftover p orbitals for?

150
00:06:40,489 --> 00:06:42,874
We can see their job in ethene,

151
00:06:42,954 --> 00:06:45,492
also called ethylene.

152
00:06:45,546 --> 00:06:48,079
It's two carbons joined by a double bond,

153
00:06:48,239 --> 00:06:50,612
meaning they share two pairs of electrons,

154
00:06:50,666 --> 00:06:54,399
and each carbon also holds two hydrogens.

155
00:06:54,479 --> 00:06:57,610
Both carbons are sp².

156
00:06:57,650 --> 00:06:59,970
Each carbon points one hybrid straight at

157
00:07:00,010 --> 00:07:02,210
the other carbon and its other two hybrids

158
00:07:02,290 --> 00:07:04,285
at its hydrogens.

159
00:07:04,325 --> 00:07:06,485
Where two orbitals overlap head on like

160
00:07:06,565 --> 00:07:09,365
that, chemists call it a sigma bond,

161
00:07:09,445 --> 00:07:12,828
and every single bond you've drawn is one.

162
00:07:12,965 --> 00:07:15,148
The spot between the two carbons is now

163
00:07:15,588 --> 00:07:18,108
taken, so the second bond has to form somewhere

164
00:07:18,188 --> 00:07:19,673
else.

165
00:07:19,713 --> 00:07:22,806
Each carbon still has its leftover p orbital,

166
00:07:22,860 --> 00:07:26,673
and those two p orbitals stand side by side.

167
00:07:26,713 --> 00:07:29,593
They overlap sideways, above and below the

168
00:07:29,953 --> 00:07:32,849
plane, and that sideways overlap is called

169
00:07:33,009 --> 00:07:35,309
a pi bond.

170
00:07:35,389 --> 00:07:38,429
So a double bond is one sigma bond plus

171
00:07:38,509 --> 00:07:40,509
one pi bond.

172
00:07:40,549 --> 00:07:43,509
A triple bond is one sigma bond plus two

173
00:07:43,629 --> 00:07:47,259
pi bonds, and every pi bond uses up one

174
00:07:47,339 --> 00:07:50,530
unmixed p orbital on each atom.

175
00:07:50,570 --> 00:07:52,010
You can read that rule backwards,

176
00:07:52,050 --> 00:07:53,415
too.

177
00:07:53,455 --> 00:07:55,855
A carbon with one double bond has to keep

178
00:07:55,935 --> 00:07:57,815
one p orbital out of the mix,

179
00:07:57,855 --> 00:08:00,045
so it's sp²...

180
00:08:00,085 --> 00:08:02,325
and a carbon with a triple bond has to keep

181
00:08:02,405 --> 00:08:05,525
two of them out, so it's plain sp.

182
00:08:05,605 --> 00:08:09,080
This is the secret of Ines's pencil.

183
00:08:09,120 --> 00:08:13,136
In graphite, every carbon is sp².

184
00:08:13,176 --> 00:08:15,256
Each carbon is bonded to three neighbors

185
00:08:15,296 --> 00:08:17,283
in a flat sheet of hexagons,

186
00:08:17,309 --> 00:08:19,951
a lot like chicken wire, and the leftover

187
00:08:20,031 --> 00:08:22,671
p orbitals overlap sideways across that

188
00:08:22,831 --> 00:08:24,397
same sheet.

189
00:08:24,534 --> 00:08:27,694
All the strong bonding stays inside a sheet,

190
00:08:27,734 --> 00:08:29,374
so when the sheets stack up,

191
00:08:29,414 --> 00:08:31,494
only weak attractions hold one sheet to

192
00:08:31,574 --> 00:08:33,100
the next.

193
00:08:33,169 --> 00:08:35,809
When she drags the tip across the paper,

194
00:08:35,889 --> 00:08:38,964
thin flakes rub off and stay behind...

195
00:08:39,004 --> 00:08:43,325
and those flakes are her gray line.

196
00:08:43,365 --> 00:08:45,540
This one's for you to try.

197
00:08:45,620 --> 00:08:49,205
A carbon atom is part of a triple bond.

198
00:08:49,285 --> 00:08:51,845
What is its hybridization?

199
00:08:51,925 --> 00:08:55,445
How many leftover p orbitals does it have?

200
00:08:55,525 --> 00:08:57,845
And what angle do its bonds make?

201
00:09:05,145 --> 00:09:07,408
A triple bond has two pi bonds,

202
00:09:07,528 --> 00:09:10,928
and each pi bond uses up one unmixed p orbital,

203
00:09:11,008 --> 00:09:13,488
so this carbon has two left over.

204
00:09:13,568 --> 00:09:16,048
That leaves the s and one p to mix,

205
00:09:16,168 --> 00:09:19,308
so the carbon is plain sp.

206
00:09:19,388 --> 00:09:22,028
Its two hybrids point in opposite directions,

207
00:09:22,108 --> 00:09:25,548
so the angle is one hundred eighty degrees.

208
00:09:25,628 --> 00:09:28,108
Most people slip by seeing the word triple

209
00:09:28,188 --> 00:09:30,588
and writing a three after the sp.

210
00:09:30,668 --> 00:09:32,588
That little number counts the p orbitals

211
00:09:32,668 --> 00:09:35,228
in the mix, and it doesn't count bonds.

212
00:09:39,028 --> 00:09:41,188
You won't redo the mixing for every atom

213
00:09:41,268 --> 00:09:44,188
you meet, because there's a shortcut,

214
00:09:44,308 --> 00:09:46,879
and it's just counting.

215
00:09:46,959 --> 00:09:48,719
You count the electron groups around the

216
00:09:48,799 --> 00:09:50,279
atom.

217
00:09:50,359 --> 00:09:53,754
A group can be a bond to a neighboring atom.

218
00:09:53,794 --> 00:09:55,754
It can also be a lone pair,

219
00:09:55,834 --> 00:09:57,834
meaning two electrons that the atom keeps

220
00:09:57,914 --> 00:09:59,700
to itself.

221
00:09:59,740 --> 00:10:02,100
And a double or triple bond is counted as

222
00:10:02,180 --> 00:10:03,940
one group.

223
00:10:04,100 --> 00:10:06,675
Only its sigma bond sits in a hybrid,

224
00:10:06,755 --> 00:10:08,995
because the pi bonds are made from the leftover

225
00:10:09,075 --> 00:10:11,355
p orbitals.

226
00:10:11,555 --> 00:10:13,945
Then you read off the answer.

227
00:10:14,025 --> 00:10:15,385
If you count four groups,

228
00:10:15,860 --> 00:10:18,591
the atom is sp³.

229
00:10:18,711 --> 00:10:21,766
With three groups, it's sp².

230
00:10:21,846 --> 00:10:23,826
And with only a pair of groups,

231
00:10:23,866 --> 00:10:25,506
it's plain sp.

232
00:10:26,222 --> 00:10:27,302
Why does that work?

233
00:10:28,013 --> 00:10:30,813
Every sigma bond and every lone pair needs

234
00:10:30,893 --> 00:10:33,238
one hybrid orbital to sit in,

235
00:10:33,318 --> 00:10:35,398
so the number of groups tells you how many

236
00:10:35,518 --> 00:10:38,719
orbitals the atom had to mix.

237
00:10:38,759 --> 00:10:41,684
We can test it on two molecules.

238
00:10:41,764 --> 00:10:44,564
Methane's carbon has four bonds and no lone

239
00:10:44,644 --> 00:10:46,039
pairs.

240
00:10:46,119 --> 00:10:50,315
That's four groups, so it's sp³.

241
00:10:50,435 --> 00:10:53,275
Carbon dioxide has one carbon with two double

242
00:10:53,315 --> 00:10:56,320
bonds, one to each oxygen.

243
00:10:56,360 --> 00:10:58,240
Each double bond counts once,

244
00:10:58,280 --> 00:11:00,760
so the carbon has two groups and it's plain

245
00:11:00,920 --> 00:11:02,625
sp.

246
00:11:02,745 --> 00:11:05,345
That fits, because two pi bonds need two

247
00:11:05,465 --> 00:11:08,380
leftover p orbitals, and the whole molecule

248
00:11:08,460 --> 00:11:10,020
is a straight line.

249
00:11:10,060 --> 00:11:12,340
Lone pairs are where most people slip,

250
00:11:12,380 --> 00:11:14,380
and they show up on atoms like nitrogen

251
00:11:14,460 --> 00:11:16,780
and oxygen, which mix their orbitals just

252
00:11:16,860 --> 00:11:18,926
as carbon does.

253
00:11:18,966 --> 00:11:21,406
In ammonia, nitrogen has three bonds to

254
00:11:21,566 --> 00:11:24,436
hydrogen and one lone pair.

255
00:11:24,516 --> 00:11:26,796
Three plus one is four groups,

256
00:11:26,836 --> 00:11:30,131
so the nitrogen is sp³.

257
00:11:30,171 --> 00:11:33,291
In water, oxygen has two bonds and two lone

258
00:11:33,371 --> 00:11:35,731
pairs, which makes four groups again,

259
00:11:35,771 --> 00:11:39,461
so water's oxygen is also sp³.

260
00:11:39,501 --> 00:11:41,061
If you forgot the lone pairs,

261
00:11:41,101 --> 00:11:44,741
you'd get the wrong answer for both of them.

262
00:11:44,861 --> 00:11:48,571
This next detail confuses a lot of students.

263
00:11:48,651 --> 00:11:52,291
Methane, ammonia and water are all sp³,

264
00:11:52,331 --> 00:11:54,811
so in each one the four groups point toward

265
00:11:54,891 --> 00:11:57,612
the corners of a tetrahedron.

266
00:11:57,652 --> 00:11:59,892
But the three molecules don't have the same

267
00:11:59,972 --> 00:12:00,852
shape.

268
00:12:00,932 --> 00:12:03,372
Shape describes where the atoms are,

269
00:12:03,412 --> 00:12:05,772
so it leaves the lone pairs out.

270
00:12:05,812 --> 00:12:07,705
Methane is a tetrahedron,

271
00:12:07,759 --> 00:12:10,212
ammonia is a shallow three sided pyramid

272
00:12:10,292 --> 00:12:13,867
with nitrogen on top, and water is bent.

273
00:12:14,027 --> 00:12:16,587
So hybridization counts every group,

274
00:12:16,667 --> 00:12:20,492
and shape only shows the atoms.

275
00:12:20,532 --> 00:12:22,852
Back in the workshop, Ines rolls her cutting

276
00:12:23,012 --> 00:12:25,317
torch over to the steel.

277
00:12:25,357 --> 00:12:27,370
One of its tanks holds acetylene,

278
00:12:27,424 --> 00:12:29,397
which is two carbons joined by a triple

279
00:12:29,677 --> 00:12:33,348
bond, with one hydrogen on each end.

280
00:12:33,388 --> 00:12:34,988
If you count the groups on one of those

281
00:12:35,108 --> 00:12:37,468
carbons, you find the bond to hydrogen and

282
00:12:37,548 --> 00:12:39,393
the triple bond.

283
00:12:39,433 --> 00:12:41,913
That makes two groups, so the carbon is

284
00:12:41,993 --> 00:12:44,148
plain sp.

285
00:12:44,188 --> 00:12:45,668
Both carbons are the same,

286
00:12:45,708 --> 00:12:49,468
so all four atoms sit in one straight line.

287
00:12:49,668 --> 00:12:51,748
She lights the torch, and the acetylene

288
00:12:51,828 --> 00:12:54,308
burns in pure oxygen at more than three

289
00:12:54,548 --> 00:12:57,344
thousand degrees Celsius.

290
00:12:57,424 --> 00:12:59,744
That flame heats the steel until a jet of

291
00:12:59,824 --> 00:13:01,904
oxygen can burn right through it,

292
00:13:02,064 --> 00:13:04,104
along the line her diamond scratched.

293
00:13:05,604 --> 00:13:07,044
It's your turn again.

294
00:13:07,124 --> 00:13:09,924
In this molecule, a carbon with three hydrogens

295
00:13:10,004 --> 00:13:12,844
is bonded to a second carbon.

296
00:13:12,964 --> 00:13:14,924
That second carbon has a triple bond to

297
00:13:15,004 --> 00:13:17,484
a nitrogen, and the nitrogen carries one

298
00:13:17,564 --> 00:13:19,549
lone pair.

299
00:13:19,629 --> 00:13:21,909
What is the hybridization of each carbon,

300
00:13:21,949 --> 00:13:23,229
and of the nitrogen?

301
00:13:32,415 --> 00:13:34,975
The first carbon has three bonds to hydrogen

302
00:13:35,135 --> 00:13:36,575
and one bond to carbon.

303
00:13:37,336 --> 00:13:40,616
That's four groups, so it's sp³.

304
00:13:41,416 --> 00:13:44,149
The second carbon is plain sp,

305
00:13:44,229 --> 00:13:46,389
because its single bond and its triple bond

306
00:13:46,549 --> 00:13:49,044
make only two groups.

307
00:13:49,084 --> 00:13:51,284
The nitrogen has the triple bond and one

308
00:13:51,364 --> 00:13:54,244
lone pair, so it has two groups and it's

309
00:13:54,324 --> 00:13:55,364
sp as well.

310
00:13:56,164 --> 00:13:58,271
The trap is forgetting the lone pair on

311
00:13:58,351 --> 00:14:00,671
the nitrogen, or counting the triple bond

312
00:14:00,751 --> 00:14:02,031
as three groups.

313
00:14:05,831 --> 00:14:08,511
We can put the whole idea in one picture.

314
00:14:09,247 --> 00:14:11,807
Carbon promotes one electron and mixes its

315
00:14:11,967 --> 00:14:14,842
orbitals, and what it mixes decides where

316
00:14:14,922 --> 00:14:16,922
its groups point.

317
00:14:16,962 --> 00:14:18,442
If you count four groups,

318
00:14:18,522 --> 00:14:21,362
the atom is sp³.

319
00:14:21,442 --> 00:14:24,478
Its groups point to the corners of a tetrahedron,

320
00:14:24,558 --> 00:14:27,403
one hundred nine point five degrees apart,

321
00:14:27,443 --> 00:14:29,723
and it keeps no p orbital back.

322
00:14:30,523 --> 00:14:32,683
Three groups make it sp²,

323
00:14:32,723 --> 00:14:34,683
so it's flat with angles of one hundred

324
00:14:34,843 --> 00:14:37,403
twenty degrees, and it has one p orbital

325
00:14:37,523 --> 00:14:40,088
left over for a pi bond.

326
00:14:40,128 --> 00:14:43,278
And a pair of groups makes it plain sp,

327
00:14:43,358 --> 00:14:45,838
a straight line at one hundred eighty degrees,

328
00:14:45,958 --> 00:14:48,798
with two p orbitals left for two pi bonds.

329
00:14:52,598 --> 00:14:55,078
By morning, the steel heron stands in the

330
00:14:55,238 --> 00:14:59,254
town square, and Ines pulls the cover off.

331
00:14:59,334 --> 00:15:02,398
Her pencil drew it, her diamond marked it,

332
00:15:02,452 --> 00:15:04,105
and her flame cut it out.

333
00:15:04,840 --> 00:15:07,133
Carbon was in all three tools,

334
00:15:07,187 --> 00:15:09,080
and what made the soft pencil and the hard

335
00:15:09,320 --> 00:15:11,560
diamond so different was how each carbon

336
00:15:11,640 --> 00:15:14,381
atom had mixed its orbitals.

337
00:15:14,421 --> 00:15:16,061
You'll find the notes and practice for this

338
00:15:16,261 --> 00:15:19,591
topic, free, on levlprep.com.

339
00:15:19,671 --> 00:15:21,751
And next time, we'll finish hybridization

340
00:15:21,871 --> 00:15:24,511
with charged carbons, the one case where

341
00:15:24,591 --> 00:15:27,071
the count misleads you, and what a hybrid's

342
00:15:27,191 --> 00:15:29,311
makeup does to the length of a bond.

